2026/05/04 11:19:09 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: When translating short declarative sentences with named people, explicitly add class facts for each named person, e.g. `(: s1 (Person bob) (STV 1.0 1.0))` and `(: s2 (Person anna) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, not only relation facts. For coordinated names like “Bob and Anna,” also add `DifferentFrom bob anna`. Resolve pronouns like “They” back to the named people instead of creating a literal `they` entity. For “Bob and Anna like hiking,” create individual facts `(: ... (Likes bob hiking) ...)` and `(: ... (Likes anna hiking) ...)`. For “They often go together,” prefer a simple reusable relation such as `GoTogether bob anna` and, if the relation is symmetric, also `GoTogether anna bob`; do not use an idiosyncratic predicate name that later questions are unlikely to reuse. When a question asks for “the name of the people/persons who like hiking,” do not add new knowledge; query using the predicates already present in context and include the person constraint, e.g. `(: $prf (And (Person $x) (Likes $x hiking)) $tv)`. When a later question says “Who goes hiking?” in a context where the only available activity evidence is that named people like hiking, do not invent an unsupported `GoesHiking` query unless the context actually contains `GoesHiking` facts or a rule deriving it. Instead, reuse `Likes $x hiking` with `Person $x` so the query can bind Bob and Anna. Preserve distinctions like ability vs current action only when the wording and context make that distinction important; for casual hobby questions, map “goes hiking” to the available hiking-preference/activity predicate rather than returning no proof.
