2026/05/04 11:33:06 INFO dspy.teleprompt.simba_utils: Advice for nl2pln.predict: If a sentence says a named person habitually does an action to a small counted set of objects, e.g. “Bob eats 5 apples every morning,” do not encode the count as a single numeric argument like `(Eats Bob apple 5 $m)`. Instead, create explicit object witnesses for the count, use lowercase stable constants, and make the habit rule derive one action fact per witness. For this example, prefer a rule like `(: r1 (Implication (Premises (Morning $m)) (Conclusions (Eats bob apple1 $m) (Eats bob apple2 $m) (Eats bob apple3 $m) (Eats bob apple4 $m) (Eats bob apple5 $m))) (STV 1.0 1.0))`, plus `Apple` facts for `apple1`...`apple5` and `DifferentFrom` facts between the apple witnesses. If a later question says morning has come today and asks whether Bob will eat 5 apples, add `(: s1 (Morning today) (STV 1.0 1.0))` and query the exact conjunction of the five derived eating facts, e.g. `(: $prf (And (Eats bob apple1 today) (Eats bob apple2 today) (Eats bob apple3 today) (Eats bob apple4 today) (Eats bob apple5 today)) $tv)`. Reuse the exact predicate names, argument order, constants, and lowercase entity spelling from the context.
